[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Navigation Performance: Screen Transitions, Memory Management, and Lazy Loading in React Native"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Building performant React Native applications requires a deep understanding of how navigation libraries handle screen lifecycles, memory allocation, and component rendering. In this tutorial, we will explore advanced strategies using React Navigation to optimize screen transitions, manage memory efficiently, and implement lazy loading."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Optimizing Screen Transitions"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Janky screen transitions often occur due to heavy JavaScript execution or complex layout calculations on the UI thread during navigation gestures. To ensure smooth 60fps or 120fps animations, enable native animations and configure heavy screens to render after the transition completes."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"<Stack.Screen
  name="Detail"
  component={DetailScreen}
  options={{
    animation: 'slide_from_right',
    freezeOnBlur: true,
  }}
/>"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Memory Management with freezeOnBlur"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"When users navigate away from a screen, React Navigation keeps the previous screen mounted by default to allow quick back transitions. However, keeping heavy component trees mounted consumes significant RAM. The freezeOnBlur option utilizes react-native-screens to completely suspend the React tree of unfocused screens, drastically lowering memory consumption."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Suspends React component rendering for unfocused screens."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Preserves navigation state without keeping views in the native hierarchy."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reduces garbage collection frequency and prevents out-of-memory crashes on lower-end Android devices."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Implementing Lazy Loading for Stack and Tab Navigators"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Lazy loading ensures that screens or heavy tab components are only initialized when the user actually navigates to them. By default, bottom tab navigators might mount all tabs simultaneously. You can optimize this by lazy loading screens or deferring heavy data fetching."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"import React, { lazy, Suspense } from 'react';
import { ActivityIndicator, View } from 'react-native';

const LazyProfileScreen = lazy(() => import('./ProfileScreen'));

function ProfileLoader(props) {
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<View style={{flex: 1, justifyContent: 'center', alignItems: 'center'}}><ActivityIndicator /></View>}>
      <LazyProfileScreen {...props} />
    </Suspense>
  );
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining native stack drivers, enabling freezeOnBlur, and incorporating React.lazy with Suspense boundaries, you can build buttery-smooth React Native applications that handle large navigation trees efficiently without memory bloat."}]
